.TH R_MEMPOOL_CREATE_FROM_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER 3 "2026-08-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer \- create ranged pool from user buffer
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cmempool.h>

r_mempool *r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(
    void *buffer,
    size_t buf_size,
    uint8_t smallest_size_power_of_two,
    uint8_t largest_size_power_of_two,
    uint8_t number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two,
    r_memory_fallback_policy_t fb_policy,
    bool single_threaded,
    ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs,
    char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a ranged memory pool using a user-provided buffer instead of
allocating sub-pool storage from the heap. The buffer is divided into
contiguous segments, one per size tier, using the same size/count
progression as r_mempool_create(). Useful for embedded systems.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
buffer                     Pointer to preallocated buffer
buf_size                   Size of buffer in bytes (must match
                          CALCULATE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER_SIZE
                          exactly)
smallest_size_power_of_two              Exponent for smallest pool size
largest_size_power_of_two               Exponent for largest pool size
number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two  Exponent for smallest pool count
fb_policy                 Fallback policy for exhausted pools
single_threaded           If true, omit locking
mmgmt_procs               Custom memory management, or NULL
err                       Optional pointer to receive error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns pointer to newly created ranged memory pool on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Buffer size must exactly match CALCULATE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER_SIZE
Buffer is not freed by r_mempool_destroy() (user manages buffer lifetime)
Pool structs are still allocated via mmgmt_procs
Buffer contains all sub-pools in adjacent segments
Returns NULL with error if buffer is not sufficiently aligned for the
  pool's internal per-element header (a size_t plus a pointer); a
  buffer declared via DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER is always
  properly aligned, a hand-rolled buffer must be aligned to at least
  _Alignof(max_align_t)
Same power-of-two size/count constraints as r_mempool_create() apply
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER (3),
.BR r_mempool_create (3),
.BR r_mempool_destroy (3)
